          "title": "Do We Need Complex Topology Control? Distinct-Peer Random Routing Improves Cost-Efficiency in Sparse Multi-Agent Debate",
          "title_zh": "我们需要复杂的拓扑控制吗？稀疏多智能体辩论中异质对等随机路由提升成本效率",
          "url": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2609.27150v1",
          "source": "arXiv",
          "section": "arxiv_other",
          "organization": "",
          "language": "en",
          "arxiv_id": "2609.27150",
          "summary": "The paper examines sparse multi-agent debate and finds that a simple random-without-replacement routing policy, in which each agent debates with two distinct and newly sampled peers every round, is a surprisingly strong baseline that consistently improves the accuracy-cost trade-off. It further shows that lightweight deliberation stopping substantially reduces inference cost while preserving competitive accuracy, and argues that sophisticated topology control should be compared against such simple routing and stopping baselines.",
